      "id": "hiretu2019l-beyza",
      "end": 1599,
      "contributors": [],
      "provenance": "Pleiades",
      "associationCertaintyURI": "https://pleiades.stoa.org/vocabularies/association-certainty/certain",
      "start": 1500,
      "details": "<p><em>\u1e24\u012bret\u00fc\u2019l-bey\u017c\u0101</em>:\u00a0This is also one of the cities of <em>\u2018Ir\u0101\u1e33</em> (Iraq), lying about one farsakh from <em>K\u016bfe</em> (Kufa).\u00a0In the age of ignorance (<em>zam\u0101n-\u0131 c\u0101hiliyye</em>), it was a highly prosperous place.\u00a0When M\u00fcn\u1e95ir bin \u00dcmer\u0101\u2019i\u2019l-\u1e32ays gained victory over his enemies in this town, he restored it and had great churches (<em>ken\u0101\u2019is-i \u2018a\u1e93\u012bme</em>) built there.\u00a0According to some, the town now called <em>Necef</em> (Najaf) stands on the site of this former settlement.\u00a0Others say that it was located on the shore of the <em>Ba\u1e25r-i F\u0101rs</em> (Persian Gulf); this, too, is reported.\u00a0It is also narrated that when one of the <em>T\u00fcbb\u0101\u2018</em> of Yemen traveled toward <em>\u1e2aor\u0101s\u0101n</em> (Khorasan), he halted at a certain spot for the night, and the next day constructed a town there and named it <em>\u1e24\u012bre</em>.\u00a0It is said that the <em>\u1e32a\u1e63r-\u0131 \u1e24averna\u1e33</em> (Palace of Khavarnaq) is also located there, which was the residence of Nu\u2018m\u0101n bin M\u00fcn\u1e95ir. [translation]</p>",
